Start();
search reset Grokking list;
register(SinkGraft);

/*****************************************************************************
    sinkGraft -- grammar minion round 1, 2026-07-30.

    Adds `cout` to incant's surface WITHOUT touching a single shared file.
    Nothing here edits incant/grammar, incant/setup or any .twk/.rtn; the new
    statement keyword is defined in this file and grafted into the live
    WardeD rule at runtime, exactly the way incant/grammarOnTheFly grafts
    DelimText into DatA.

    Tony's ruling (kantCorpus CLAIM KANT-13): ONE print mechanism, several
    destinations. PrintXP+ is fixed; the keyword selects only the sink. So
    CoutXP is PrinT term-for-term, with one substitution: the keyword.

    Why the keyword field is named pCout and not cout
    -------------------------------------------------
    aCTionPrinT picks its sink with ONE character. ruleActions.rtn:665 reads
        if command.tag == 'p'   return opPrint(input,buffer);
        else                    return opString(command,buffer);
    and GroupRules.mm:709 shows what that compiles to:
        if ( *command->groupBody->tag == 'p' )
    -- a first-character test on the tag of the rule's FIRST LABEL. A keyword
    field literally named `cout` starts with 'c', so it would silently take
    the opString branch and build a value instead of printing. Naming the
    field pCout and giving it the surface text "cout" (the loopOnAttributes=
    "attributes" shape, incant/grammar:135) puts a 'p' where the fork looks
    while the user still types `cout`.

    That is a HACK and it is deliberately visible: it is the evidence that
    sink selection is a first-character test on a field NAME. See
    docs/grammarCorpus.md, CLAIM GRAM-2.

    Why the ruleMethod is attached in a SECOND define
    ------------------------------------------------
    A bare reference to a method-bearing field in an expression INVOKES it.
    With `ruleMethod=aCTionPrinT` on the rule at definition time,
    `WardeD += CoutXP;` calls aCTionPrinT and adds its numeric result to
    WardeD instead of adding the rule. Defining the rule plain, grafting it,
    then re-opening the name to attach the method sidesteps that entirely.
    See docs/grammarCorpus.md, CLAIM GRAM-1.
*****************************************************************************/

define
    CoutXP isRule pCout="cout" followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PrintXP+ SemI-;
    CerrXP isRule cerr="cerr"  followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PrintXP+ SemI-;
    ;

WardeD += CoutXP;
WardeD += CerrXP;

define
    CoutXP ruleMethod=aCTionPrinT defer;
    CerrXP ruleMethod=aCTionPrinT defer;
    ;
guard(WardeD);

print ``"G1 grafted CoutXP + CerrXP into WardeD":;

/*****************************************************************************
    THE ORACLE. `print` is the fixture for `cout` -- same PrintXP, same
    expected bytes. The two blocks below are character-identical apart from
    the keyword; genLadder-style extraction and cmp does the comparing.
*****************************************************************************/
print ``"--ORACLE-print--":;
print "alpha" 1 2 3:;
print $"tight" _ "spaced":;
print ``"backtick literal" "then plain":;
print "num" 42 " end":;
print ,+"bunched shortcut reaches +":;
print "trailing colon newline":;
print ``"--ORACLE-cout--":;
cout "alpha" 1 2 3:;
cout $"tight" _ "spaced":;
cout ``"backtick literal" "then plain":;
cout "num" 42 " end":;
cout ,+"bunched shortcut reaches +":;
cout "trailing colon newline":;
print ``"--ORACLE-end--":;

print ``"--CERR-begin--":;
cerr "this line asked for stderr":;
print ``"--CERR-end--":;

stop();
